hey guys it's vadim with maxtec and over  the last couple of days benchmarks for  apple's brand new m1 pro and m1 max  chips have been leaked and at first  glance they kind of look a bit  disappointing because they don't seem to  match up to our initial estimates and  predictions but the truth is actually  hidden in real world performance which i  will show you later so in this video i'm  gonna go through all of the leaked  benchmarks including ones for both the  m1 pro and m1 max chips and i'm also  going to compare them to other mac and  windows pc systems and then i'm going to  also look at some more realistic leaked  tests that show the true power of these  chips so getting right into the leaked  geekbench 5 benchmarks let's start with  the cpu side before going over to the  gpu as you can see we have some slight  variation in the scores with the highest  one giving us an astounding 1700  single core and 12  693 multi-core points now that score is  definitely not what we expected because  i personally predicted around 14 000  and a half multi-core points but then  again apple did confirm that the m1 pro  has a cpu that's just 70 percent faster  which almost perfectly lines up to the  leaked scores we're seeing here now the  big question is why we're not seeing in  almost doubling in performance like we  thought we would but before i dig deep  into that i want to compare those leaked  scores to other systems we actually have  a 12 core xeon mac pro in the office  which was able to score 12 518  multi-core points so the new m1 pro and  max chips are actually outperforming it  with just eight high performance cores  instead of 12 on the mac pro but making  things even crazier we measured the peak  wattage of the mac pro while running  geekbench 5 and it had a staggering  218 watts at one point in the test and  according to apple's own comparison  charts the new chips are limited to 30  watts of power so if you translate the  scores into performance per watt metrics  these new macbook pros are giving us  over seven times more performance per  watt which is mind-blowing now comparing  the league score to the previous fully  specced out 16-inch intel macbook pro  the new one is literally almost twice as  fast and going further one of my twitter  followers shared this image of a bunch  of intel and amd server chips and as you  can see the new m1 mac chips are faster  than almost all of them including the 32  core amd epic 7542 and another twitter  follower johnny mcclung who has his own  youtube channel shared the scores of his  16 core xeon mac pro and it was just  barely faster than apple's new 10 core  chip and finally comparing it to the  best x86 laptop ship out there the intel  i9 11 980 hk apple's new chip is much  faster so even though we're not getting  the performance we expected these new  macbook pros are still the fastest  laptops ever made but nevertheless the  truth is that apple is still limiting  these chips with a maximum of 30 watts  of power and we know that it's not due  to thermal throttling because geekbench  5 is a quick test that never really lets  the cpus heat up that much so it's got  to be a hard 30 watt cap by apple now  why would they do this well for two  different reasons one reason is because  of amdahl's law which basically means  that you get diminishing returns when  adding more cores for running programs  with parallel processing now the other  reason is probably because apple showed  that the 32 core m1 matched gpu uses 60  watts of power in their comparison  charts to other windows gpus so if you  add that 60 watts from the gpu to the 30  watts from the cpu you get a total of 90  watts of power usage if you're fully  maxing out both of them at the same time  which isn't likely to happen but it  still might for very intense workloads  and then if you consider the fact that  the best power adapter option available  with the 14 inch macbook pro is a 96  watt charger that would leave only 6  watts of extra power for the rest of the  system if you were theoretically maxing  out both the cpu and the gpu so apple  likely put a 96 watt total power cap on  the entire system to ensure that the  battery won't drain while you're  charging it so now the 30 watt power  limit on the cpu starts to make perfect  sense now before i get into all of the  gpu benchmark leaks the story for the  cpu doesn't end just yet the apple  developer steve moser was looking  through the beta of mac os monterey and  he found a brand new high power mode  setting which is the opposite of low  power mode steve ended up finding this  very interesting text from apple  themselves your mac will optimize  performance to better support resource  intensive tasks  this may result in louder fan noise so  that's great and all but the caveat is  that this mode is only available on the  16-inch macbook pro and only if you have  the m1 max chip which is very  interesting because the 14 inch can also  be configured with the same fully  specced out m1 max so with that said  what we have to figure out is what is so  special about the 16 inch model well the  16 inch is obviously much larger in  terms of the chassis size as well as  being thicker even though it packs the  same chip configurations which means  that the 16 inch has more internal space  to work with and yes the larger 100 watt  hour battery compared to 70 on the 14  inch does take up some room but there's  still some left over space so our theory  is that the 16 inch model with the m1  max chip actually comes with a better  cooling system and if you need some  proof for that theory here you go if you  look at the tech specs on the 16-inch  macbook pro the model with the m1 max  chip is actually heavier than the one  with the regular m1 pro chip so you  might be thinking that the difference is  explained by just the larger chip  however if you look at the 14 inch  model's tech specs there is no  difference in weight  between the two chips so what if you  truly do get a better cooling system on  the 16 inch model with the m1 max now  with that said another theory for this  16 inch model exclusively getting high  power mode is actually due to the power  adapter since the 14 inch model can only  have up to a 96 watt power adapter what  if apple will not allow the high power  mode on that machine because of the hard  96 watt power cap but they will on the  16 inch because it comes with an 140  watt power adapter which can recharge  faster who knows but either way the  point that i'm trying to make is that  there's still a chance we could see  better scores with high power mode  enabled if those leaked benchmarks were  not using it but what i can assure you  is that we will for sure be testing out  the new high power mode when we get in  our eight different macbook pro  configurations so be sure to subscribe  right now so you don't miss out and by  the way we just launched our brand new  m1 max tech t-shirt design which you can  find in the merge shelf below as well as  this sharpie edition design that i'm  wearing right now and be sure to use the  promo code m1 pro to get 20 off on all  of our merch now getting into the league  graphics benchmark scores the highest  one we saw was 68  870 points from that 32 core m1 max gpu  using the optimized metal framework now  at first glance that seems extremely  disappointing because i was personally  expecting around 80 to 90 000 graphics  points in this test so in reality this  leaked score doesn't seem that much  faster than the 42 500 points that the  previous intel macbook pros 5600 mgpu  scored and it's nowhere near the  performance of the fastest laptop gpu  which is the 3080 mobile which apple  compared the m1 max ship to in one of  their comparison charts but in reality  apple's new chips pack a ton of unique  and record-breaking hardware components  inside of them including an absolutely  insane 32 channels of memory and a very  impressive 400 gigabytes per second of  memory bandwidth so these raw gpu  benchmarks don't show the whole truth so  before i get into how this 32 core gpu  performs in the real world including  some more leaked tests i want to get  into the lower end 16 core gpu and the  less expensive m1 pro chip the best car  we got for this chip is 41  914 which is actually super impressive  and it's just barely shy of our  performance expectations being almost  exactly two times faster than the m1s 8  chord gpu which would mean it's getting  almost perfect scaling however the  really weird thing is that this leaked  score is actually lower than last year's  top of the line 5600 m gpu and this is  what finally brings us to the real world  leaked tests that show a totally  different picture in gfx bench 5.0 you  can now see that the 16 core m1 pro  outperforms the 5600m and basically  every test that we see here and some of  these are by huge margins like as much  as 50 faster which proves that the  geekbench drive benchmark isn't showing  us the full truth and even in apple's  own testing like 3d rendering using  maxon cinema 4d with redshift apple  shows the 16 core m1 pro being a  staggering two and a half times faster  than the 5600m and yes apple's notes  show that the 5600m was fully loaded  even with 64 gigabytes of ram and an 8  terabyte ssd now with that said you may  have noticed that on the same 3d  rendering chart from apple the m1 max  only gives you four times more  performance instead of the expected five  times if it's scaled perfectly so apple  is admitting that yes the 32 core gpu  has diminishing returns compared to the  16 core but don't worry because that  leaked 68 870 geekbench score isn't  telling you the truth because we just  got a set of leaked gfx bench benchmarks  and as you can see  there are many tests where this new 32  core m1 max gpu is actually  outperforming the 3080 mobile laptop gpu  because these tests are taking full  advantage of the m1 max chip package so  in reality do not trust those  disappointing looking geekbench scores  because it performs much better in real  world use cases so with all of that said  we can see that you're not getting as  much bang for your buck with the m1 max  chip compared to the m1 pro but that has  honestly always been the case with  graphics cards and it's exactly what we  expected so if you want the best value  get the m1 pro but if you want the  absolute most graphics power you can get  go for the 32 core m1 max so hopefully  you enjoyed this video and if you did go  ahead and click the circle above to  subscribe for more videos like this one  and definitely check out our buyer's  guide video right over there thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  one  [Music]
 

